course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 11
title: The flag, and what's cooking at the friend's house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Sashko's family the zhovto-blakYtnyi flag, yellow and blue, is a serious symbol of pride, so it is not something to make jokes about.
explain: Lunch at Granny's often starts with borshch, a red beetroot soup with a spoon of smetAna, sour cream — the family's pride.
explain: Then maybe vareNYky, little dumplings you can help pinch shut, filled with potato, cheese, cabbage — or, surprise, sweet cherries.
explain: Many food words are traps for English ears: kompOt is a drink, not a dessert; syrnYky are made with soft cottage cheese, not cheddar; holubtsI means «little doves» but has no birds inside.
fact: zhovto-blakYtnyi means yellow-and-blue, said with pride about the Ukrainian flag, a serious symbol in the family.
fact: borshch is a red beetroot soup served with sour cream; in 2022 UNESCO listed the culture of Ukrainian borshch cooking as heritage in need of urgent protection, though similar soups are eaten in neighbouring countries too.
fact: vareNYky are dumplings filled with potato, cheese, cabbage or cherries, often made together at the table; cherry vareNYky are sweet.
fact: pelmeni are a different dumpling, with meat, and are not the same as vareNYky.
fact: holubtsI are cabbage rolls with rice and meat; the name means «little doves», but there are no birds inside.
fact: smetAna is sour cream, put on borshch, vareNYky and pancakes; it is sour, not sweet like whipped cream.
fact: sAlo is cured pork fat sliced on bread; grandads love it, people joke about it as a Ukrainian food, and a guest can politely say ni, DYAkuyu.
fact: mlyntsI are thin pancakes eaten with jam or cottage cheese; rolled with a filling they may be called nalysnyky.
fact: syrnYky are fritters of soft cottage cheese, eaten with smetAna or jam; syr means cheese, but here it is soft cottage cheese.
fact: khlib means bread, often dark rye; it is respected, and grannies frown on dropping or throwing it.
fact: kartOplya means potato, and kOvbasa is a salami-like sausage, while a hot-dog sausage is a sosyska.
fact: kompOt is a home drink of fruit and berries boiled in a big pot, not the English dessert «compote».
exercise: What does zhovto-blakYtnyi describe? | The yellow-and-blue Ukrainian flag | A yellow and blue sweet | A football team's socks | A kind of borshch
exercise: Why should a guest not joke about the zhovto-blakYtnyi flag? | It is a serious symbol of pride for the family | It is against the rules of football | It brings bad luck to the whole house for a year | It is a secret
exercise: What is borshch? | A red beetroot soup, with sour cream | A meat pie | A sweet cake with poppy seeds | A cabbage salad with grated carrot and oil
exercise: What did UNESCO do about borshch in 2022? | Listed Ukrainian borshch cooking as heritage needing urgent protection | Banned it in schools | Named it the world's best soup | Said it comes from France
exercise: What are vareNYky? | Dumplings with potato, cheese or cherries | Thin pancakes rolled with jam and cream | Cabbage rolls | Baked buns stuffed with minced meat and onion
exercise: How are vareNYky different from pelmeni? | pelmeni are a different, meat dumpling | They are exactly the same dish | pelmeni are pancakes | vareNYky are always meat
exercise: What are holubtsI? | Cabbage rolls with rice and meat | Roasted pigeons | Little round doughnuts with jam inside | Dumplings with cherries
exercise: What does smetAna taste like? | Sour, it is sour cream | Sweet, like whipped cream | Salty, like cheese | Like vanilla ice cream
exercise: What is sAlo? | Cured pork fat, sliced on bread | A kind of salad with lettuce and egg | Salt water | A sweet roll
exercise: What can a guest say if he doesn't want sAlo? | ni, DYAkuyu | nE-a, fu, gross | proBAch, pani | SMAchno, duzhe
exercise: What are mlyntsI? | Thin pancakes | Small river fish | Mint sweets, humbugs | Potato cakes, fried
exercise: What are syrnYky made from? | Soft cottage cheese | Grated cheddar cheese | Potatoes | Apples
exercise: What does khlib mean? | Bread | A cake | Butter | Some soup
exercise: How do grannies feel about throwing bread? | They frown on it; bread is respected | They find it funny | They do it for luck at New Year | They don't mind at all, it's only bread
exercise: What does kartOplya mean? | Potato | A carrot | Cabbage | A farm cart
exercise: What is kOvbasa? | A salami-like sausage | A hot dog | A thick cabbage soup with beans | A kind of cake
exercise: What is kompOt? | A home drink of boiled fruit and berries | A fruit dessert in a bowl | A compost heap at the bottom of the garden | A kind of jam
test: «compote is a pudding, so kompOt is pudding» Which is right? | No, kompOt is a fruit drink | Yes, it is a pudding eaten after dinner | Yes, it is jelly | No, it is soup
test: «syrnyky = cheese, so cheddar on toast» Which is right? | No, syrnYky are fritters of soft cottage cheese | Yes, cheese on toast | Yes, a cheddar sandwich | No, they are thin potato chips fried in sunflower oil
test: «holubtsi have doves in them, gross» What is right? | No, the name means «little doves», but they are cabbage rolls | Yes, real doves | Yes, but only at Easter | No, the name means «little doves», but they are filled with fish
test: You bite a vareNYk and it's full of sweet cherries. What's going on? | Cherry vareNYky are sweet, quite normal | It has gone wrong, the cook mixed up the jars | It is a joke on you | It's pelmeni
test: Granny offers sAlo and you don't fancy it. What would happen if you said «ni, DYAkuyu» politely? | That's fine — not liking it is okay | She'd be deeply insulted for ever | She would make you eat it all up anyway | She would ask you to leave
test: «borshch is Russian» says a kid on the bus. What would Sashko's family most likely say? | That it is Ukrainian, and UNESCO listed Ukrainian borshch cooking in 2022 | That they agree completely | That it is Italian | That borshch is a dessert
test: «chuck the bread at the pigeons, nobody cares» says a boy at Granny's table. What will Granny most likely think? | That it's wrong: bread is respected | That it's a great game for the grandchildren | That bread is for pigeons only | Nothing, she'll join in
test: You want a hot-dog sausage, not salami. Which word? | sosyska | kOvbasa | kartOplya | khlib
test: «pour the smetAna on, it's like cream» your mate says. What will it taste like? | Sour, not sweet | Sweet like whipped cream | Like vanilla custard | Like chocolate sauce
test: «mlyntsI, nalysnyky, same thing or what» What is right? | Both are thin pancakes; rolled with a filling they may be called nalysnyky | They are different soups | nalysnyky are dumplings | mlyntsI are bread rolls
test: In a cartoon the hero eats borshch with whipped cream and sugar. What would be more usual? | smetAna, sour cream | Ice cream and maple syrup | Chocolate sprinkles on top | A spoon of strawberry jam
exam: Identify the beverage designated by the term kompOt. | A domestic drink of fruit and berries boiled together | A dessert of stewed fruit served in a bowl with a spoon | A fermented dairy product | A variety of tea
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning borshch and UNESCO. | In 2022 the culture of Ukrainian borshch cooking was inscribed as heritage in need of urgent safeguarding | UNESCO prohibited the preparation of borshch | UNESCO declared borshch to be of French origin | UNESCO has never considered borshch
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises holubtsI? | They are rolls of cabbage filled with rice and meat, the name signifying «little doves» | They are roasted doves | They are dumplings filled with cherries | They are thin pancakes rolled with cheese
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct of a guest who declines the offer of sAlo. | A courteous refusal such as ni, DYAkuyu, which is entirely acceptable | Immediate departure from the table | Consumption of the dish under protest | An expression of disgust
